insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$Close the pump today, prove it next week$seed$, $seed$My first recommendation is to take the handle off the public pump today and warn households supplied by the river company to boil their water.

The pattern points to water more than air. Cases cluster in a few streets, but the workhouse sits in the same neighbourhood, breathes the same air, and has almost no cases. Its difference is its own well. If foul air were the cause, the workhouse should suffer too.

I am not certain, and closing the pump will anger people who depend on it, so I would pair it with free water carts from the spring-fed company. The cost of being wrong is a few weeks of inconvenience; the cost of waiting is more deaths.

To test the idea, my clerks will visit every household with a case and record where it gets its water and when the illness began. If water is the cause, death rates should be much higher for the river company's customers than for the spring company's, even in the same streets. I will report rates, not counts, since the companies serve different numbers of houses.

One caution: if cases fall after the pump closes, that alone does not prove my case, because outbreaks often fade by themselves. The comparison of water companies is the stronger test.$seed$, true, $seed$Act on the Water$seed$, 1
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-outbreak'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$Close the pump today, prove it next week$seed$);
insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$Get the numbers first, then act decisively$seed$, $seed$Before asking the council to do anything costly, I would spend two or three days gathering evidence, because a wrong decision will cost us their trust for the rest of the outbreak.

My clerks would list every case with address, date of onset, occupation and water source. I would plot them on a street map and by day. Then I would calculate the rate of illness for each water source and for households near and far from the sewer.

The two theories predict different things. If the air is to blame, illness should follow distance from the sewer and tannery regardless of water. If the water is to blame, households in the same street should differ depending on their supplier, and outlying cases should trace back to the river company or the pump.

The risk is that people die while we count. To limit that, I would immediately issue advice that costs nothing: boil drinking water and keep the sick apart from food preparation. Then, with the data, I can ask for the expensive measures with confidence.$seed$, true, $seed$Investigate to Decide$seed$, 2
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-outbreak'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$Get the numbers first, then act decisively$seed$);
insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$Cheap measures against both theories$seed$, $seed$The council is split, and I cannot settle the argument in a day. So I would recommend measures that are cheap and help whichever theory is right, and design them so they also teach us something.

Against the water theory: close the public pump and supply clean water by cart. Against the air theory: clear the open sewer near the worst streets. Both are affordable and can be done this week.

Crucially, I would not do everything everywhere at once. If the sewer is cleared in one district first while the pump closes town-wide, and illness falls everywhere, that suggests water. Meanwhile my clerks would compare death rates among the two water companies' customers.

The difficulty is politics. The river company's director will resist any suggestion that his water kills. I would present rates, not accusations, and ask the council to commit in advance to acting on whatever the comparison shows.$seed$, true, $seed$Cover Both Explanations$seed$, 3
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-outbreak'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$Cheap measures against both theories$seed$);
-- Day 3: The Mountain Pass
select public._save_challenge($seed${"number":3,"slug":"the-mountain-pass","title":"The Mountain Pass","summary":"Two valleys divided by a mountain range need a railway between them. Choose how to cross the mountains, and defend the route you would build.","subjects":["Engineering","Geography","Logistics"],"scenario_kind":"fictional","scenario_note":"A fictional Alpine-style region in the 1870s. The valleys, company and figures are invented; the engineering choices are the real ones railway builders faced.","role":{"title":"The Chief Engineer","body":"A new railway company has hired you to connect two prosperous valleys separated by a mountain range. Its investors want an answer this season: which way should the line cross?"},"situation":"The valleys sit about 30 miles apart as the crow flies, but the range between them rises nearly 4,000 feet above the valley floors. Today goods cross a high pass by mule and cart, and the pass closes for four to five months each winter. A river gorge winds around the end of the range, adding roughly 40 miles, and floods each spring. Surveyors have identified three possibilities: a line climbing to the pass, a long tunnel beneath it, or a line through the gorge.","final_task":"Recommend a route and a method for crossing the mountains, and defend it. Explain how it handles gradient, winter, cost, construction time and the traffic the railway must carry.","expectations":{"easy":"Recommend a route and explain clearly why it suits the problem better than the alternatives.","moderate":"Compare the routes on gradient, cost, time to build and year-round reliability, justify your choice, and explain the trade-offs.","hard":"Evaluate the routes against each other, address the major uncertainties (geology, weather, traffic forecasts), consider long-term operating costs and second-order effects on the region, and defend your choice against the strongest case for an alternative."},"cards":[{"kind":"role","image":{"key":"survey","alt":"A surveyor's theodolite on a tripod beside rolled maps."}},{"kind":"situation","image":{"key":"pass","alt":"A road zig-zagging up a steep mountainside to a high pass."}},{"kind":"constraints","image":{"key":"avalanche","alt":"Snow-covered slopes above a narrow track, with an avalanche path cut through the forest."}},{"kind":"investigation","image":null},{"kind":"decision","image":{"key":"pass","alt":"A road zig-zagging up a steep mountainside to a high pass."}}],"constraints":[{"label":"Steep gradients","detail":"Ordinary locomotives lose grip and power on steep slopes.","icon":"trending-up"},{"label":"Winter snow and avalanches","detail":"The pass is closed for months; slopes above it slide every winter.","icon":"snowflake"},{"label":"Investors' money and patience","detail":"The company can raise a lot, but not without limit, and wants returns.","icon":"coins"},{"label":"Unknown rock","detail":"Nobody knows exactly what lies inside the mountain.","icon":"pickaxe"}],"investigation":[{"title":"Engineering","questions":["How steep can a railway be, and how have engineers gained height on mountain lines?","What makes long tunnels difficult, slow and dangerous to build?"]},{"title":"Geography","questions":["How do snow, avalanches and spring floods affect each route?","What does each route mean for the towns and land along it?"]},{"title":"Logistics","questions":["How much traffic must the line carry, and how fast and how reliably?","How do construction time, building cost and running costs compare between the routes?"]}],"reference":{"background":"Gradient: conventional (adhesion) railways rely on friction between wheel and rail. Main lines are usually kept to gentle gradients (often under about 2.5%), and steep mountain main lines rarely exceed roughly 3-4%. Steeper climbs need rack (cog) railways, which are slow and carry limited loads, or cable systems.\nGaining height: engineers lengthen the route to reduce the gradient, using curves along valley sides, horseshoe curves, spiral (helical) tunnels, and zig-zag switchbacks (which require trains to reverse, limiting speed and capacity). The Gotthard line in Switzerland (opened 1882) used spiral tunnels on its approaches.\nSummit vs base tunnels: a tunnel beneath a pass removes the highest, snowiest section. 19th-century examples include the Fréjus (Mont Cenis) tunnel, opened 1871, and the Gotthard tunnel, about 15 km, built 1872-1882 at great cost and with the deaths of many workers. Long tunnels faced unknown geology, water inflows, heat, ventilation problems and slow progress; the compressed-air rock drill and dynamite (1860s) sped construction. A lower, longer tunnel shortens the climb but costs more and takes longer.\nWinter: lines over high passes face snow and avalanches; protections include snow sheds/galleries, avalanche barriers, and choosing routes on less exposed slopes, but closures can still occur. Rivers in gorges flood in spring snowmelt; lines need embankments, bridges and protection, and gorge routes can face rockfall.\nEconomics: building cost vs running cost. A longer, flatter route can cost more to build but less to operate (lighter locomotives, heavier trains), while a short steep route may need extra locomotives (banking engines) on every train. Reliability through winter often matters as much as speed for freight. Phased approaches (e.g., a cheaper line first, improved later) and the risk of cost overruns on tunnels are real considerations.","approaches":[{"title":"Tunnel beneath the pass","summary":"Accept a long, costly build for a shorter, gentler, year-round line with high capacity."},{"title":"Gorge route","summary":"Avoid the mountain: longer but gentler and quicker to build, with flood and rockfall protection."},{"title":"Climb to the pass","summary":"Use loops, spirals or rack sections and snow sheds for a cheaper, faster-to-build line, accepting winter risk and lower capacity."},{"title":"Phased","summary":"Build the gorge or climbing line first to earn revenue, and plan a tunnel later if traffic justifies it."}],"misconceptions":["That trains can climb gradients similar to roads.","That a tunnel's cost and duration can be predicted precisely before the rock is known.","Comparing routes only by distance rather than by gradient, reliability and operating cost.","That snow sheds or avalanche defences make a high route fully reliable."],"evaluator_notes":"Any of the routes can be defended. Credit responses that reason about gradient (and why it matters for rail), winter reliability, construction time and cost, and the traffic the line must serve. Strong hard-level responses treat the tunnel's geology as genuine uncertainty, distinguish building cost from operating cost, and consider phasing. Do not require exact historical figures; correct clear errors such as assuming ordinary trains can climb 10% grades.","sources":[{"title":"Histories of the Gotthard and Mont Cenis (Fréjus) railway tunnels","note":"Construction methods, timelines, costs and dangers."},{"title":"Introductory railway engineering texts on adhesion and ruling gradients","note":"Why railways need gentle slopes."}]}}$seed$::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('id', (select id from public.challenges where slug = 'the-mountain-pass')), null);
insert into public.challenge_schedule (release_date, challenge_id)
  select public.app_today() + 2, id from public.challenges where slug = 'the-mountain-pass'
