insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$Pay once for the tunnel$seed$, $seed$I recommend a long tunnel beneath the pass, approached by gentle loops on each side.

A railway is only valuable if it runs all year. The pass line would close or be disrupted for months every winter, exactly when the valleys most need supplies. Avalanche sheds help, but they cannot make a 4,000-foot climb safe and reliable. The gorge line avoids the mountain but floods every spring and adds 40 miles to every journey forever.

The tunnel's problem is cost and uncertainty. Nobody knows the rock, and tunnels of this era took around a decade. I would begin with test borings at both ends and drive from both sides at once with modern rock drills. I would also tell the investors honestly that the cost estimate could rise substantially.

The trade-off is a slower return. But over decades, a short, gentle, year-round line means lighter locomotives, longer trains and lower running costs, which is where a railway makes or loses money.$seed$, true, $seed$The Tunnel$seed$, 1
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-mountain-pass'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$Pay once for the tunnel$seed$);
insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$Go around, and open in three years$seed$, $seed$The gorge route is longer, but it is the one I am confident we can build on time and on budget.

Its gradients are gentle because it follows the river, so ordinary locomotives can haul full trains. It avoids the snow at the pass and the unknown rock inside the mountain, the two largest risks in the other plans. Floods are a known problem with known answers: raise the line on embankments, build bridges above the highest flood marks, and protect the banks.

The extra 40 miles cost time and fuel on every train. I accept that, because a working railway in three years earns money and goodwill, while a tunnel might take ten and could bankrupt the company.

If traffic grows enough to justify it, the company can build a tunnel later, paid for by the revenue this line earns.$seed$, true, $seed$The Gorge$seed$, 2
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-mountain-pass'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$Go around, and open in three years$seed$);
insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$Climb now, tunnel later$seed$, $seed$I would build the line over the pass first, then tunnel beneath its summit once the railway is earning.

The climbing line is the cheapest and quickest way to connect the valleys. Spirals and horseshoe curves can keep the gradient manageable, and a short summit tunnel avoids the very top. Snow sheds over the worst avalanche paths will keep it open most of the winter.

It will not be perfect: heavy trains will need a second locomotive on the climb, and severe storms will still close it. But it proves the traffic exists, which makes it far easier to raise money for a long base tunnel later.

The risk is that the company never finds the money for the tunnel and the region is stuck with a slow line. I would ask the investors to set aside a share of profits for it from the start.$seed$, true, $seed$Phased Approach$seed$, 3
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-mountain-pass'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$Climb now, tunnel later$seed$);
-- Day 4: The Failing Expedition
select public._save_challenge($seed${"number":4,"slug":"the-failing-expedition","title":"The Failing Expedition","summary":"Your polar expedition is running out of food and time, and one of your party is injured. Decide what the group should do, and how you will lead them through it.","subjects":["Leadership","Physiology","Resource Management"],"scenario_kind":"fictional","scenario_note":"A fictional polar expedition in the early 20th century. The party and its situation are invented; the physiology and the history it draws on are real.","role":{"title":"The Expedition Leader","body":"You lead a party of eight hauling sledges across a polar ice sheet. Every person's life depends on the decisions you make in the next few days, and they will look to you for how you make them."},"situation":"A storm has pinned your party in its tents for six days. You are about 180 miles from your base hut and 70 miles from a supply depot laid last season, if it is still there. You have food for about 18 days at full rations and paraffin to melt snow for about 14. One man has a badly sprained ankle and cannot pull a sledge. Two others show early frostbite. The weather is improving, but the season is turning colder each week.","final_task":"Decide what the party should do (press on to the depot, return to base, split up, or wait) and explain how you will manage food, fuel, health and morale along the way. Defend your plan and its risks.","expectations":{"easy":"Choose a plan and explain why it gives the party the best chance of survival.","moderate":"Compare the main options, justify your choice with the numbers you have (distances, food, fuel, pace), and explain the trade-offs and risks.","hard":"Evaluate the options under uncertainty (the depot may be gone, the weather may turn), plan for what would make you change course, consider the physiology of cold and hunger, and address the human side of leading a frightened group, including the ethics of splitting up."},"cards":[{"kind":"role","image":{"key":"polar-camp","alt":"Two canvas tents and a loaded sledge on an ice sheet under a low sun."}},{"kind":"situation","image":{"key":"ice-field","alt":"A field of jagged pressure ridges and drifting snow stretching to the horizon."}},{"kind":"constraints","image":{"key":"rations","alt":"Wooden supply boxes, a paraffin tin and a ration tally on a sledge."}},{"kind":"investigation","image":null},{"kind":"decision","image":{"key":"polar-camp","alt":"Two canvas tents and a loaded sledge on an ice sheet under a low sun."}}],"constraints":[{"label":"Food for 18 days, fuel for 14","detail":"Without fuel you cannot melt snow to drink.","icon":"package"},{"label":"An injured member","detail":"He cannot pull, and someone must help him or carry him.","icon":"heart-pulse"},{"label":"Worsening cold","detail":"Each week brings lower temperatures and shorter days.","icon":"thermometer-snowflake"},{"label":"A frightened team","detail":"Fear, exhaustion and disagreement can break a group.","icon":"users"}],"investigation":[{"title":"Physiology","questions":["How much energy does hauling a sledge in extreme cold demand, and what happens when people eat less than they burn?","How do cold, dehydration and injury affect what a person can do?"]},{"title":"Resource Management","questions":["How far can the party travel each day, and do the food and fuel last the distance under each plan?","What could you leave behind, ration or reorganise to change the numbers?"]},{"title":"Leadership","questions":["How have expedition leaders kept groups together in desperate situations, and what went wrong when they failed?","How should a leader make and explain a decision that puts some people at greater risk than others?"]}],"reference":{"background":"Energy: man-hauling sledges in polar cold is among the most energy-demanding sustained work humans do; estimates for polar sledging commonly run around 5,000-7,000 kcal per day or more, and historical rations often provided far less, producing steady weight loss and weakness. Reduced rations extend the time food lasts but reduce strength and pace and increase vulnerability to cold.\nWater and fuel: on ice, drinking water comes from melting snow, which needs fuel; running out of fuel causes dehydration even with food remaining. Dehydration worsens fatigue and frostbite risk.\nCold injury: hypothermia impairs judgement and coordination; frostbite risk rises with wind, wet clothing, dehydration and poor circulation; injuries slow the whole party.\nPace: loaded man-hauling parties often managed somewhere around 8-15 miles a day in reasonable conditions, much less in soft snow, rough ice or bad weather. Carrying or helping an injured person reduces pace.\nThe arithmetic in the scenario: 70 miles to the depot at ~10 miles/day is about 7 days, well within 14 days of fuel and 18 of food, but if the depot is missing the party would then be ~110+ miles from base with roughly half its fuel gone. Returning 180 miles at ~10 miles/day takes about 18 days, beyond the fuel supply unless pace improves or fuel is rationed; at a slower pace with the injured man, it is worse. Splitting the party (a fast group to the depot to bring supplies back, or to fetch help) can improve the arithmetic but adds risks to both groups and raises ethical questions.\nHistorical comparisons: Ernest Shackleton's Endurance expedition (1914-1917) is often cited for leadership that kept every member alive through extreme hardship; Robert Falcon Scott's party died returning from the South Pole in 1912, with short rations, fuel shortages at depots, cold and injury all contributing. These are comparisons, not part of the scenario.\nLeadership: clear decision-making, honest communication, fair sharing of hardship, keeping the group together, contingency plans with explicit decision points, and maintaining routine and morale are commonly identified factors in survival situations.","approaches":[{"title":"Press on to the depot","summary":"Shortest distance to resupply; risk if the depot is missing, so set a turnaround point and ration fuel from now."},{"title":"Return to base","summary":"Certain destination but the fuel arithmetic is tight; requires strict rationing and a faster pace."},{"title":"Split the party","summary":"A strong group goes ahead to the depot or base to bring back supplies or help while others follow or wait; faster but risky and ethically fraught."},{"title":"Wait for better conditions","summary":"Rest and recover, but food and fuel keep burning while the season worsens; generally weak unless rescue is expected."}],"misconceptions":["Treating food as the only limiting resource and ignoring fuel for water.","Assuming the party can maintain its normal pace with an injured member and reduced rations.","Assuming the depot is certainly there.","Ignoring morale and cohesion as practical survival factors."],"evaluator_notes":"There is no single correct choice; the depot plan is strong on arithmetic but needs a contingency, and other plans can be well defended. Credit responses that do the arithmetic (distance, pace, days of food and fuel), recognise fuel as the binding constraint, plan for uncertainty (a turnaround point or decision rule), and address the injured member and morale. For splitting, credit explicit discussion of its risks and ethics. Do not require knowledge of specific historical expeditions.","sources":[{"title":"Histories of the Endurance (1914-1917) and Terra Nova (1910-1913) expeditions","note":"Leadership, rations and survival in polar conditions."},{"title":"Introductory texts on exercise physiology in cold environments","note":"Energy expenditure, hypothermia and frostbite."}]}}$seed$::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('id', (select id from public.challenges where slug = 'the-failing-expedition')), null);
insert into public.challenge_schedule (release_date, challenge_id)
  select public.app_today() + 3, id from public.challenges where slug = 'the-failing-expedition'
  on conflict do nothing;
update public.challenges set status = 'published' where slug = 'the-failing-expedition' and status = 'draft';
insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$To the depot, with a line in the snow$seed$, $seed$We go for the depot, together, with a firm rule about when to turn back.

The numbers favour it. Seventy miles at about ten miles a day is seven days, within both our food (18 days) and our fuel (14 days). Returning to base is 180 miles: eighteen days at that pace, more with the injured man, and our fuel runs out first. Waiting burns fuel and the season gets colder.

The risk is that the depot is gone. So we cut fuel use now (one hot meal a day, melting snow only for drinking) and set a rule: if we have not found it by day nine, we turn for base on whatever we have left. Everyone hears the rule before we leave.

The injured man rides on a sledge, which slows us, but leaving him is not an option I will consider. The two with frostbite rotate to the lighter sledge. I will tell the team the plan, the numbers and the turnaround rule plainly. Fear grows in silence.$seed$, true, $seed$Press On, With a Limit$seed$, 1
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-failing-expedition'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$To the depot, with a line in the snow$seed$);
insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$Send the strongest ahead$seed$, $seed$I would split the party: three of the fittest go to the depot light and fast, and return with fuel; the other five follow at the injured man's pace.

Fuel is our real limit. A light party could cover fifteen miles a day and reach the depot in five days. If the depot is there, they bring fuel back to meet the main group, which by then has covered half the distance. If it is not, they return with the bad news quickly, and we turn for base together with more time than if we had all walked there.

Splitting is dangerous, and history is full of parties that separated and never met again. We would agree a route, marker cairns and a meeting rule before leaving, and the advance party would carry its own tent and full equipment.

I would lead the slower group myself. The person asking others to take a risk should not take the easier role.$seed$, true, $seed$Split the Party$seed$, 2
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-failing-expedition'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$Send the strongest ahead$seed$);
insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$Back to the hut we know$seed$, $seed$I would turn for base. It is far, but it is certain, and certainty matters when lives depend on it.

The depot was laid last season. Snow, wind and ice may have buried it, and if we march seventy miles to find nothing, we will be more than a hundred miles from the hut with half our fuel gone. That is the disaster I most want to avoid.

The fuel arithmetic for the return is tight, so we must change it. From today: one hot meal a day, melt snow only for drinking, and leave behind every non-essential item to lighten the sledges and keep our pace above ten miles a day.

I would explain my reasoning to the team, and ask for objections before we leave. If the weather stays clear and we make good progress, we keep going; if it turns, we reconsider the depot.$seed$, true, $seed$Return to Base$seed$, 3
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-failing-expedition'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$Back to the hut we know$seed$);
-- Day 5: The Energy Dilemma
select public._save_challenge($seed${"number":5,"slug":"the-energy-dilemma","title":"The Energy Dilemma","summary":"An island's only large power station is closing in six years. Decide what should replace it, keeping the lights on, bills affordable and emissions falling.","subjects":["Physics","Economics","Environmental Science"],"scenario_kind":"hypothetical","scenario_note":"A hypothetical island region in the present day. The island and its figures are invented; the technologies, costs and trade-offs are real.","role":{"title":"Head of the Energy Authority","body":"You run the island's energy authority. The island's government will follow your recommendation, and its 400,000 residents will live with the result: their bills, their air and their winter evenings."},"situation":"The island's coal-fired power station supplies about 70% of its electricity and must close in six years: it is old, expensive to repair, and the island has committed to cutting emissions. Demand peaks on cold, still winter evenings, and is rising as homes switch to electric heating and cars. The island is windy, has some sun, a long coastline, and sits 60 miles from a mainland grid. Residents already pay more for electricity than mainland households.","final_task":"Recommend what should replace the power station by the time it closes, and defend your plan. Explain how it keeps power reliable through winter peaks, what it costs and who pays, and how it affects emissions.","expectations":{"easy":"Recommend a replacement and explain clearly why it would work for the island.","moderate":"Compare at least two options on reliability, cost and emissions, justify your recommendation, and explain its main trade-offs.","hard":"Evaluate a mix of options, address uncertainty (prices, demand growth, construction delays), explain how the system copes with a still, cold week, consider second-order effects (on jobs, land, the grid and future demand), and defend your plan against its strongest objection."},"cards":[{"kind":"role","image":{"key":"pylons","alt":"Electricity pylons and cables crossing open country."}},{"kind":"situation","image":{"key":"power-station","alt":"An old power station with tall chimneys and cooling towers beside the sea."}},{"kind":"constraints","image":{"key":"wind-coast","alt":"Wind turbines along a rocky coastline under a wide sky."}},{"kind":"investigation","image":null},{"kind":"decision","image":{"key":"wind-coast","alt":"Wind turbines along a rocky coastline under a wide sky."}}],"constraints":[{"label":"Six years until closure","detail":"Whatever you choose must be built and working in time.","icon":"hourglass"},{"label":"Winter evening peaks","detail":"The highest demand comes when it is cold, dark and often still.","icon":"zap"},{"label":"Bills already high","detail":"Many households struggle to pay; investment costs reach them eventually.","icon":"coins"},{"label":"Emissions commitments","detail":"The island has promised to cut its emissions steeply.","icon":"leaf"}],"investigation":[{"title":"Physics","questions":["How much electricity do wind turbines and solar panels produce on average, and how does that vary by hour and season?","What can batteries, other storage and grid connections do, and for how long?"]},{"title":"Economics","questions":["How do the costs of building and running each option compare, and who pays for them?","How should you compare an option that is cheap to run but expensive to build with one that is the reverse?"]},{"title":"Environmental Science","questions":["What are the emissions and land or sea impacts of each option, over its whole life?","What happens to emissions as heating and transport switch to electricity?"]}],"reference":{"background":"Variability: wind and solar output depends on weather; their average output relative to capacity (capacity factor) is roughly 25-45% for wind (offshore at the higher end) and roughly 10-25% for solar depending on latitude and climate. Solar output is lowest in winter evenings, exactly when demand peaks in a cold climate. Extended cold, still periods (sometimes called \"Dunkelflaute\") are a key reliability challenge.\nStorage: lithium-ion batteries are well suited to shifting energy by hours (commonly 1-4 hours of discharge) and to fast grid services, but are not economic for covering multi-day or seasonal gaps at scale today. Pumped hydro offers longer storage where geography allows. Hydrogen and other long-duration storage exist but are less mature and less efficient.\nFirm capacity: something must cover a still, cold week: dispatchable generation (gas turbines, which emit CO2 but can run rarely as backup; biomass; nuclear), an interconnector to a larger grid, demand flexibility, or long-duration storage. A system with mostly wind and solar plus a backup that runs rarely can cut emissions steeply while keeping reliability.\nInterconnectors: a subsea cable to the mainland shares resources and backup; costs are substantial and depend on length and capacity; projects can take many years to plan and build, and the island depends on mainland supply and prices during shortages.\nNuclear: large reactors have very low operating emissions but long construction times (often a decade or more) and high capital costs; small modular reactors are promoted as faster and cheaper but are not yet widely proven commercially, so a six-year timeline is very uncertain.\nGas: combined-cycle gas plants emit roughly half the CO2 of coal per unit of electricity; open-cycle gas turbines are cheaper to build and suitable for occasional peaks; gas depends on fuel imports and prices.\nEconomics: levelised cost (cost per unit of energy over a lifetime) favours wind and solar in many places today, but whole-system cost also includes backup, storage, grid upgrades and balancing. Capital-intensive options shift costs to the present and depend on interest rates; fuel-based options expose consumers to price volatility. Who pays (taxpayers, bill-payers, investors) and how costs are spread matter for fairness.\nDemand side: efficiency, insulation, smart charging, time-of-use pricing and flexible heating can reduce peaks; electrification of heat and transport raises total demand and winter peaks. Closing a plant affects local jobs; transition planning is a real consideration.","approaches":[{"title":"Renewables with firm backup","summary":"Large wind (and some solar) build-out, batteries for hours, and a gas plant kept for rare still, cold spells, with a plan to cut its use over time."},{"title":"Interconnector-led","summary":"A cable to the mainland plus local wind; shares backup across a larger grid, but depends on the mainland and takes years."},{"title":"Nuclear","summary":"Firm low-carbon power, but very unlikely within six years; needs a bridge solution."},{"title":"Demand-first","summary":"Cut and shift demand through efficiency and flexibility to shrink the peak before building supply."}],"misconceptions":["Treating installed capacity as average output (ignoring capacity factors).","Assuming batteries can cover multi-day still periods economically.","Assuming a new nuclear plant could reliably be built within six years.","Ignoring that electrification raises winter peak demand.","Comparing only generation cost while ignoring backup, storage and grid costs."],"evaluator_notes":"Several plans can be well defended. Credit responses that address the winter peak and still periods explicitly, distinguish capacity from output, deal with the six-year timeline, and consider cost and who pays as well as emissions. Hard-level responses should handle uncertainty and second-order effects (jobs, grid, rising demand). Do not require precise figures; correct clear physical or economic errors.","sources":[{"title":"International Energy Agency (IEA) reports on renewables, storage and system integration","note":"Capacity factors, costs and flexibility."},{"title":"Lazard Levelized Cost of Energy+ analyses","note":"Comparative generation and storage costs (US-based)."}]}}$seed$::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('id', (select id from public.challenges where slug = 'the-energy-dilemma')), null);
insert into public.challenge_schedule (release_date, challenge_id)
  select public.app_today() + 4, id from public.challenges where slug = 'the-energy-dilemma'
  on conflict do nothing;
update public.challenges set status = 'published' where slug = 'the-energy-dilemma' and status = 'draft';
insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$Wind first, gas only when it must$seed$, $seed$I recommend building offshore and onshore wind to supply most of the island's electricity, with batteries for daily balancing and a small gas plant that runs only during long still, cold periods.

The island is windy, and wind is now among the cheapest new sources of electricity. Turbines produce on average perhaps a third to under half of their capacity, so we must build roughly two to three times the old plant's capacity, but that is achievable in six years.

The hard part is the winter evening with no wind. Batteries cover a few hours, not a week. Rather than pretend otherwise, I would keep a gas plant that runs perhaps a few percent of the year, which still cuts emissions sharply compared with coal.

Costs will raise bills at first, so I would fund construction through long-term contracts at fixed prices and protect low-income households with targeted support.$seed$, true, $seed$Renewables With Backup$seed$, 1
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-energy-dilemma'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$Wind first, gas only when it must$seed$);
insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$Connect the island to the mainland$seed$, $seed$The island's problem is that it must be self-sufficient on its worst day. A cable to the mainland removes that problem.

With a 60-mile subsea connection, the island can import power on still winter evenings and export its surplus wind on stormy days. A larger grid has more variety of weather and more backup, so the island no longer needs to build its own plant for a rare week.

The risks are time and dependence. Cables like this can take many years to approve and build, so I would start immediately and keep the old plant on standby for a year or two if needed. During mainland shortages, the island would pay mainland prices.

Combined with new wind on the island, the cable gives a cleaner and more reliable system than either could alone.$seed$, true, $seed$The Interconnector$seed$, 2
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-energy-dilemma'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$Connect the island to the mainland$seed$);
insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$Shrink the peak before building for it$seed$, $seed$Every option is expensive because it must cover the winter evening peak. So my first recommendation is to make that peak smaller.

Insulating homes, time-of-use prices, smart charging for cars and heat pumps that pre-heat homes in the afternoon can move demand away from the evening. Each megawatt of peak we avoid is a megawatt we never have to build.

That alone will not replace 70% of our electricity. Alongside it I would build wind and batteries, and procure firm backup for rare still spells. But the backup can be smaller and cheaper if the peak is lower.

The risk is that demand measures depend on thousands of household decisions and may deliver less than planned, so I would track results each year and adjust the supply plan accordingly.$seed$, true, $seed$Demand First$seed$, 3
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-energy-dilemma'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$Shrink the peak before building for it$seed$);
